> The secret to this dish is the balance between aged black vinegar and rock sugar. The vinegar tenderizes the pork while adding a complex tanginess, and the sugar creates a beautiful caramelized glaze. Don't rush the braising—this dish rewards patience.

== Prepare the Pork ==

Rinse @pork knuckles{1%kg} (or pork trotters, cut into pieces) thoroughly.

Place in a #pot{} with @water{} to cover. Bring to a boil for ~{5%minutes} to remove impurities.

Drain, rinse under cold water, and set aside.

== Make the Sauce ==

In a large #wok{} or claypot, combine @black vinegar{250%ml} (aged), @rock sugar{100%g}, @light soy sauce{60%ml}, @dark soy sauce{30%ml}, and @water{500%ml}.

Stir in @ginger{5%slices}, @garlic{3%cloves}(crushed), and @star anise{2}.

== Braising ==

Add the blanched @pork knuckles{1%kg} to the sauce.

Bring to a boil over high heat, then reduce to a low simmer.

Cover and braise for ~{90%minutes}, turning the pork occasionally. The pork is ready when the meat is very tender and nearly falling off the bone.

== Finishing ==

Uncover the pot and increase heat slightly. 

Add @hard-boiled eggs{4}(peeled) to the braise and cook for another ~{15%minutes}, allowing them to absorb the tangy sauce and turn a deep brown color.

Continue reducing the sauce until it becomes thick, syrupy, and glossy. The sauce should coat the back of a spoon.

== Serve ==

Arrange the @pork knuckles{1%kg} on a platter. Place the @hard-boiled eggs{4} alongside.

Drizzle the reduced sauce generously over everything.

Garnish with @sesame seeds{1%tsp} and @spring onion{1%stalk}(finely sliced).

Serve hot with steamed rice—the sauce is especially delicious over rice.